---
title: v0.4.2a: making Hide play the exit animation
---
date: 2026-04-14
---
author: Labyricorn
---
summary: A Chromium animation-pairing quirk made the Hide button cut instead of animate out, fixed by cancelling in-flight animations first.
---
tags: html, interface
---
source_commit: c78872fc6067a5ecdb5b08d574037b535325259e
---
body:

Packs that chain their exit in CSS, with `animation-name: in-anim, out-anim` and
a delay on the second, animated correctly when the on-screen timer expired but
not when the operator pressed Hide. The element cut or faded abruptly instead.

The cause was in how Chromium pairs animations between the old and new style: by
index in the `animation-name` list. Adding `.tf-hiding` changed the list from
`[in, out]` to `[out]`, and the browser kept the delay that had been scheduled
for the old second slot, so no exit animation visibly played. After the hide
duration the element simply snapped to `display: none`.

The fix is in the composer. The default hide path now cancels in-flight CSS
animations on the element's subtree by setting `animation: none` and forcing a
reflow before `.tf-hiding` is added. The new rule is then treated as a fresh
animation set starting from frame zero. CSS transitions are unaffected.

Every element in the `lower-thirds-pack` and `lower-thirds-extras` packs was
confirmed affected and is fixed. Packs whose script calls `preventDefault()` on the hide event own their
exit entirely and were not changed.

This entry was written from the project's changelog.

[Commit c78872f](https://github.com/Labyricorn/TitleFlow/commit/c78872fc6067a5ecdb5b08d574037b535325259e)
